<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<UserControl
    x:Class="MeetingTranscriber.App.Configuracion"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:local="using:MeetingTranscriber.App"
    xmlns:loc="using:MeetingTranscriber.Presentation"
    xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
    xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
    mc:Ignorable="d">

    <!-- The one screen that lives apart, and everything on it is an answer about this install
         rather than about the next meeting. Four blocks, in the artboard's own order: what should
         happen when a recording ends, what runs it, who is using the application and in which
         language, and where the corpus is.

         The first block is the whole reason this screen exists. Choosing between transcribing and
         summarising stopped being a question per meeting and became a preference set once, so it
         is asked here, before any recording, and nothing asks again — which is what makes the
         presses on a meeting's own row load-bearing rather than a convenience.

         Neither engine is a picker. `docs/design.md` says there is one transcription engine and one
         summariser, so neither is a choice: what is drawn is the answer with its cost beside it,
         and a *Descargar* that downloads nothing is the live control that does nothing this
         repository refuses outright — so the row for a model that does not exist is not drawn at
         all.

         A sub-screen of the window and not a window of its own, the way the meeting screen and the
         filing screen are: it is reached from the gear on the front door and returns there, and
         a meeting under way keeps its strip above it for the whole of that. -->

    <Grid x:Name="Root" RowSpacing="18">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            <RowDefinition Height="*" />
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- The header, in the shape the meeting screen's is. The way back does not name where back
             is, and that is the one place this differs from its siblings: the meeting screen is
             reached from the list and returns to it, and this one is reached from wherever the
             window happened to be. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="0" ColumnSpacing="12">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

            <Button
                x:Name="BackButton"
                Grid.Column="0"
                VerticalAlignment="Center"
                Style="{StaticResource ButtonAtTheMargin}"
                Click="OnBack"
                Content="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.BackFromSettings)}" />

            <TextBlock
                Grid.Column="1"
                VerticalAlignment="Center"
                Text="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.Settings)}"
                Style="{StaticResource SubScreenTitleText}" />
        </Grid>

        <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="1" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
            <StackPanel Spacing="18" MaxWidth="760" HorizontalAlignment="Left">
                <!-- What happens when a recording ends: three options, one chosen, on tarjeta. A
                     set of more than two is a radio row, and every part of how one is drawn is
                     Olivo's `Option`.

                     The group goes dead when the corpus is not reachable, which is the rule the
                     row about who is using the application already applies one block down: the
                     answer has nowhere to be kept, the refusal is already on screen naming the
                     folder, and a press that would fail is worse than one that is plainly not
                     available. -->
                <Border Style="{StaticResource Card}" Padding="{StaticResource LargeCardPadding}">
                    <RadioButtons
                        x:Name="AfterARecordingOptions"
                        Header="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.WhenARecordingEnds)}">
                        <RadioButton
                            x:Name="AfterTranscribeAndSummarise"
                            Style="{StaticResource Option}"
                            Checked="OnAfterARecordingChosen"
                            Content="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.TranscribeItAndSummariseIt)}" />
                        <RadioButton
                            x:Name="AfterTranscribe"
                            Style="{StaticResource Option}"
                            Checked="OnAfterARecordingChosen"
                            Content="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.OnlyTranscribeIt)}" />
                        <RadioButton
                            x:Name="AfterNothing"
                            Style="{StaticResource Option}"
                            Checked="OnAfterARecordingChosen"
                            Content="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.DoNothingWhenItEnds)}" />
                    </RadioButtons>
                </Border>

                <!-- The two engines, side by side, each on its own card because they are charged
                     separately: if one of the two fails the other is already done and is not paid
                     for again, which is the sentence at the foot of this screen.

                     Written out and not built from a list, which is the one thing about this block
                     worth arguing. `docs/design.md` says a model on this machine is one more row in
                     both lists rather than a special case — and it is: one more row here is one
                     more pair of lines here. A loop over a list of one would be preparation for a
                     shape nobody has drawn, and the shape is already known to be different, because
                     a local model brings a size, a download and a state none of these two have. -->
                <Grid ColumnSpacing="18">
                    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                    <Border Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource Card}" Padding="{StaticResource SmallCardPadding}">
                        <StackPanel Spacing="8">
                            <TextBlock
                                Text="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.TranscribeWith)}"
                                Style="{StaticResource BodyText}"
                                Foreground="{StaticResource SecondaryTextBrush}" />
                            <StackPanel Spacing="2">
                                <TextBlock
                                    Text="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.TheEngineThatTranscribes)}"
                                    Style="{StaticResource BodyText}" />

                                <!-- The data rank, in a quieter ink: until a run produces a number
                                     this is `docs/design.md`'s placeholder standing where a figure
                                     will go, and a placeholder is not a sentence. -->
                                <TextBlock
                                    Text="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.WhatAMinuteCosts)}"
                                    Style="{StaticResource DataText}"
                                    Foreground="{StaticResource SecondaryTextBrush}" />
                            </StackPanel>
                        </StackPanel>
                    </Border>

                    <Border Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource Card}" Padding="{StaticResource SmallCardPadding}">
                        <StackPanel Spacing="8">
                            <TextBlock
                                Text="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.SummariseWith)}"
                                Style="{StaticResource BodyText}"
                                Foreground="{StaticResource SecondaryTextBrush}" />
                            <StackPanel Spacing="2">
                                <TextBlock
                                    Text="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.TheEngineThatSummarises)}"
                                    Style="{StaticResource BodyText}" />
                                <TextBlock
                                    Text="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.WhatItCosts)}"
                                    Style="{StaticResource DataText}"
                                    Foreground="{StaticResource SecondaryTextBrush}" />
                            </StackPanel>
                        </StackPanel>
                    </Border>
                </Grid>

                <!-- Who is using the application, and the language it is read in. Moved here off
                     the front door whole, handlers and all: both are answers about this install,
                     and the front door only carried them until this screen existed.

                     It is one field and one press whether it is being answered for the first time
                     or corrected afterwards — the asking is the sentence under it, which is on
                     screen exactly while nobody has answered. The affiliation the design asks about
                     is not here and is not missing: `docs/design.md` says the first screen of an
                     application is not where somebody enumerates their jobs, and the `Persona`
                     dialogue is where one is added. -->
                <StackPanel Spacing="4">
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                        <TextBox
                            x:Name="WhoIsUsingThisBox"
                            MinWidth="240"
                            Style="{StaticResource TypedField}"
                            Header="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.WhoIsUsingTheApplication)}"
                            TextChanged="OnWhoIsUsingThisTyped" />
                        <Button
                            x:Name="WhoIsUsingThisButton"
                            VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                            Style="{StaticResource NormalButtonOnPaper}"
                            Click="OnKeepWhoIsUsingThis"
                            Content="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.Save)}" />

                        <!-- The language the application is **read** in, which is not the language
                             a meeting is spoken in: that one is on the recording card, beside what
                             it is about.

                             Its label is beside it and not over it: a picker is a pill and a pill
                             carries no header, so what it chooses is said as text. That text is
                             also what a screen reader is given, by name. -->
                        <TextBlock
                            VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                            Margin="0,0,0,7"
                            Text="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.Language)}"
                            Style="{StaticResource BodyText}"
                            Foreground="{StaticResource SecondaryTextBrush}" />
                        <ComboBox
                            x:Name="LanguagePicker"
                            MinWidth="150"
                            VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                            Style="{StaticResource DropDown}"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.Language)}"
                            SelectionChanged="OnLanguageChosen" />
                    </StackPanel>

                    <!-- Left and not merely bounded. A `MaxWidth` on a block that stretches centres
                         it, so this sentence stood in the middle of a window nothing else on the
                         row was under — which a probe photographed. -->
                    <TextBlock
                        x:Name="NobodyHasSaidYet"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                        Text="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.NobodyHasSaidWhoIsUsingThis)}"
                        Style="{StaticResource BodyText}"
                        Foreground="{StaticResource SecondaryTextBrush}"
                        TextWrapping="Wrap"
                        MaxWidth="640"
                        Visibility="Collapsed" />
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- Where the corpus is, and which folder was refused when there is nowhere. This
                     is the only line in the application that says which refusal it was, and the
                     meetings list's own sentence — `UiTexts.TheCorpusCouldNotBeOpened` — sends
                     somebody to this screen for it. It came here off the recording card with the
                     row above, which is why that entry now names this screen and not that one.

                     A sentence, and so the body rank in a quieter ink. The path inside it is data
                     and the line around it is not — `docs/design.md` §Type: text never goes in
                     mono, and a number that is part of a sentence is text.

                     *Cambiar* is drawn on the artboard and is not here: relocating a corpus is a
                     folder picker nothing in this application has yet, and a button that opens
                     nothing is the live control that does nothing. So the line names the folder and
                     offers nothing. -->
                <StackPanel Spacing="4">
                    <TextBlock
                        Text="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.WhereItIsKept)}"
                        Style="{StaticResource BodyText}"
                        Foreground="{StaticResource SecondaryTextBrush}" />
                    <TextBlock
                        x:Name="CorpusText"
                        Style="{StaticResource BodyText}"
                        Foreground="{StaticResource SecondaryTextBrush}"
                        TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                </StackPanel>

                <TextBlock
                    Text="{x:Bind In(loc:UiTexts.EachIsPaidForSeparately)}"
                    Style="{StaticResource BodyText}"
                    Foreground="{StaticResource TertiaryTextBrush}"
                    TextWrapping="Wrap" />
            </StackPanel>
        </ScrollViewer>

        <!-- Said where it happened rather than in a notice, which is what the filing screen already
             does: a corpus that would not open and an answer that was refused are things about this
             screen, and the two the design allows to stop a screen are a charge and adding a
             person. -->
        <TextBlock
            x:Name="StatusText"
            Grid.Row="2"
            Style="{StaticResource BodyText}"
            Foreground="{StaticResource PeakBrush}"
            TextWrapping="Wrap"
            Visibility="Collapsed" />
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
